2026-07-13T05:15:31.0371014Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0371284Z     The ``pytest`` framework makes it easy to write small tests, yet
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0372055Z     scales to support complex functional testing for applications and libraries.
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0372472Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0372674Z     An example of a simple test:
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0372922Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0373116Z     .. code-block:: python
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0373344Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0373538Z         # content of test_sample.py
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0373797Z         def inc(x):
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0374015Z             return x + 1
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0374231Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0374409Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0374597Z         def test_answer():
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0374835Z             assert inc(3) == 5
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0375066Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0375244Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0375429Z     To execute it::
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0375634Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0375815Z         $ pytest
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0376095Z         ============================= test session starts =============================
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0376455Z         collected 1 items
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0376671Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0385014Z         test_sample.py F
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0385309Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0385754Z         ================================== FAILURES ===================================
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0386218Z         _________________________________ test_answer _________________________________
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0386572Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0386762Z             def test_answer():
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0387015Z         >       assert inc(3) == 5
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0387279Z         E       assert 4 == 5
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0387523Z         E        +  where 4 = inc(3)
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0387774Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0387987Z         test_sample.py:5: AssertionError
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0388346Z         ========================== 1 failed in 0.04 seconds ===========================
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0388816Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0388987Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0389843Z     Thanks to ``pytest``'s detailed assertion introspection, you can simply use plain ``assert`` statements. See `getting-started <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/getting-started.html#our-first-test-run>`_ for more examples.
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0390775Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0390946Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0391131Z     Features
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0391324Z     --------
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0391505Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0392404Z     - Detailed info on failing `assert statements <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/assert.html>`_ (no need to remember ``self.assert*`` names)
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0393078Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0393267Z     - `Auto-discovery
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0393707Z       <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/goodpractices.html#python-test-discovery>`_
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0394204Z       of test modules and functions
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0394468Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0394827Z     - `Modular fixtures <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/explanation/fixtures.html>`_ for
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0395361Z       managing small or parametrized long-lived test resources
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0395691Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0396049Z     - Can run `unittest <https://docs.pytest.org/en/stable/how-to/unittest.html>`_ (or trial)
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0396501Z       test suites out of the box
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0396750Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0396938Z     - Python 3.10+ or PyPy3
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0397169Z     
2026-07-13T05:15:31.0397748Z     - Rich plugin architecture, with over 1300+ `external plugins <https://docs.pytest.org/en/latest/reference/plugin_list.html>`_ and thriving community
